WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:02.600
All cops are bastards

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:08.000
tell me
something right now that the police are

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.280
good at other than whooping ass a lot of
us were asked if we could imagine a

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:14.559
future without police
and i answered yes to that question one

00:00:14.559 --> 00:00:18.000
even broader idea that's gaining
momentum right now is defunding the

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.119
police
instead of abolish the police or defund

00:00:21.119 --> 00:00:24.400
the police
how about privatize the police you know

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:29.039
policing is not
a marketplace you can't choose another

00:00:29.039 --> 00:00:31.199
police force to take
care of you to watch over your

00:00:31.199 --> 00:00:36.079
neighborhood actually private policing
is more common than most people realize

00:00:36.079 --> 00:00:39.520
and it's a proven way of making law
enforcement more accountable to the

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:44.160
communities they're paid to protect
a lot of people think that law

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:49.920
enforcement specifically
must be provided by a monopoly

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:54.800
from the government but it turns out in
history and even in modern times there's

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:59.680
plenty of private
examples of people working to create

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:03.840
order and safety in society edward
stringham is the president of the

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:08.080
american institute for economic research
and the author of private governance

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:10.720
creating order and economic and social
life

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:13.920
if you look closely in many cases you
see

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:17.840
lots of private sources of order that
goes all the way

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:21.520
from simple things like unarmed security
guards

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:24.640
it goes all the way up through very
advanced sets of

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:31.119
private legal self-regulatory
organizations so certain places like

00:01:31.119 --> 00:01:36.079
harvard university
mit massachusetts general hospital

00:01:36.079 --> 00:01:40.960
have fully deputized private police
stringham also points to what happened

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:46.240
in san francisco during the gold rush
they actually didn't have a police force

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:50.159
at
all so by the late 1840s

00:01:50.159 --> 00:01:54.560
early 1850s they started creating a
small

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:57.680
government police force but they were
basically

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:04.079
powerless and so at the same time
the private citizen said we need to

00:02:04.079 --> 00:02:07.360
create
our own private police force in

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:10.879
chinatown
the government police were not very into

00:02:10.879 --> 00:02:13.680
helping
the residents of chinatown and they also

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:17.120
relied
on private policing so this is something

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:20.560
that
is not just abstract and theoretical but

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:25.040
something that has been around
in san francisco since the very early

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:28.800
days
private governance is pervasive

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:32.160
it's all around us in history and in
modern times

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:38.080
and the better it works the less
we see it we don't police people we

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:42.239
protect them
police are law enforcement officers so

00:02:42.239 --> 00:02:46.080
essentially
their task is based on negative metrics

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:50.080
arresting people for drugs
or violence that has already occurred

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:53.360
which is not protection
dale brown is the founder of detroit

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:56.319
threat management centers
which has been providing private

00:02:56.319 --> 00:03:01.760
protection in the motor city since 1995.
we find ways to protect people even from

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:04.640
prosecution
essentially we create conditions where

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:07.599
no violence occurs and no prosecution
occurs

00:03:07.599 --> 00:03:10.720
because no violence occurs detroit
threat management centers

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:14.720
operates as a for-profit security
company that provides bodyguards works

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:18.640
with homeowners associations and secures
precious cargo delivery

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:22.959
but it also runs an educational academy
in which graduates volunteer to provide

00:03:22.959 --> 00:03:26.799
free security to domestic violence
victims and other vulnerable individuals

00:03:26.799 --> 00:03:31.519
who detroit city police don't protect
we hunt predators out of the community

00:03:31.519 --> 00:03:35.519
we deny them the opportunity
to rape rob and kill the families that's

00:03:35.519 --> 00:03:39.519
our primary function
in 2017 detroit threat management center

00:03:39.519 --> 00:03:43.680
employees stopped carrying guns
you really want to avoid the use of

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:49.040
force and violence whenever possible
it's positive it's appropriate it's

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:52.879
what creates a better quality of life
for everyone one of the main problems

00:03:52.879 --> 00:03:56.799
that we see
with bureaucracy and monopoly is

00:03:56.799 --> 00:04:02.640
a lack of concern for consumer
well-being well accountability and

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:06.080
responsibility
are intrinsic in the private sector we

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:08.239
have real-time audio and video in each
vehicle

00:04:08.239 --> 00:04:11.760
they're actually all watching each other
while they're on duty and i train my

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:14.480
staff members
constantly and then you have to know law

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:17.840
so we constantly study the law
we go through scenarios and they're

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:20.880
constantly coached
into better performance so i don't have

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:23.759
better people
i have a system and that system is what

00:04:23.759 --> 00:04:28.960
makes us capable we already have
more private security in various forms

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:32.960
in the united states than we have
government police

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:40.000
and we can think about ways that we can
continue to expand in that direction

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:43.840
for those people who are worried about
militarized or

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:47.840
violent police and thinking about ways
to

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:51.600
replace them with alternatives we don't
need to

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:56.240
dream up some abstract ideals and think
about how things might

00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:00.320
be we could actually look at how private
security

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:07.039
how private policing already exists
draw from best practices and say look

00:05:07.039 --> 00:05:13.199
we do have markets and we can rely
more on markets and less on a coercive

00:05:13.199 --> 00:05:22.400
government monopoly

